<article class="env-lemma" id="045M">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/045M">Lemma <span data-tag="045M">10.167.1</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field and let $K/k$ and $L/k$ be two field extensions such that one of them is a field extension of finite type. Then $K \otimes _ k L$ is a Noetherian Cohen-Macaulay ring. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The ring $K \otimes _ k L$ is Noetherian by Lemma <a href="/tag/045I" data-tag="045I">10.31.8</a>. Say $K$ is a finite extension of the purely transcendental extension $k(t_1, \ldots , t_ r)$. Then $k(t_1, \ldots , t_ r) \otimes _ k L \to K \otimes _ k L$ is a finite free ring map. By Lemma <a href="/tag/00R5" data-tag="00R5">10.112.9</a> it suffices to show that $k(t_1, \ldots , t_ r) \otimes _ k L$ is Cohen-Macaulay. This is clear because it is a localization of the polynomial ring $L[t_1, \ldots , t_ r]$. (See for example Lemma <a href="/tag/00ND" data-tag="00ND">10.104.7</a> for the fact that a polynomial ring is Cohen-Macaulay.) 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>